    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The western Grand Canyon provides a rare opportunity to directly observe normal faults and associated structures over large vertical cross sections with nearly complete exposure. Detailed mapping and mechanical modeling of these structures can improve our understanding of deformation associated with normal faults and thereby improve our ability to infer hydrocarbon migration pathways and to predict the occurrence of hydrocarbon traps in regions of continental extension. GPS measurements of deformed sandstones within the upper Esplanade fm. are used to evaluate both kinematic and mechanical models. Inclined shear, a widely used kinematic model, can reproduce the observed hanging wall fold shape with fault geometries that are permitted by field observations. This model, however fails to predict the observed footwall deformation. Simple elastic dislocation models can fit both footwall and hanging wall shapes and are consistent with field observations that suggest nearly planar high-angle faults to depths of ~0.5 km. 3D fold geometry and field studies of secondary deformation patterns (joints and small faults) may help to further evaluate these models by constraining the relationship between slip magnitude and fold shape and strain magnitudes and orientations, respectively. Finally, more complex mechanical models need to be evaluated to understand how elastic strains may relax, leading to an apparently “elastic” fold shape preserved over geologic time.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
        },
